--- BEGIN ISSUE ---
Confusion about the meaning of the -L parameter with D compilers
Hi!
The dmd and ldc D compilers define the `-L` flag as "pass the next command to the linker", while gdc/gcc define it as "set linker search path".
So I thought I was smart when translating a passed-in `-L` to a ldc/dmd compiler to `-L-L`, since this will make it work expected from a gcc compiler (= `-L` always sets the linker search path).
Unfortunately it now turns out that many people use the `-L` flag as "pass to linker" and even use it in existing pkg-config files, which makes it really hard to change
So, I wonder whether I should do some black magic to detect this in the DCompiler classes, or whether we could somehow utilize `get_linker_search_args()` to set linker search paths in a way all compilers understand, and make the to_native argument translation simply not-deal with an already existing `-L` flag (= have the compiler interpret it as it sees fit).
Possible uses for `-L` for setting the linker search path is to search for local, precompiled shared or static libraries, e.g. `-Lpath/to/libraries -lfoo` (to find a `libfoo.a` or `libfoo.so` file which doesn't exist system-wide). For using `-L` directly, people use it to e.g. pass linker arguments to a compiler if they expect the compiler to be LDC/DMD. E.g. `-L-lfoo` to link against libfoo.
I would prefer to just leave the `-L` flag alone in meson and utilize the `get_linker_search_args` command somehow to allow for a compiler-agnostic way to set linker search paths.

